3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/f3165b8b46c8ac0ab798ae6162d4b4facb92367f5072598bef936801bce44fc4.png)" 13194139536062 false] [17592186381694 1017 #edb/ref 17592186381695 13194139536062 true] [17592186381694 1089 "q-113769" 13194139536062 true] [17592186381694 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536062 true] [17592186381694 1100 "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}$ on the closed interval $[-1,1].$" 13194139536062 true] [17592186381694 1113 #uuid "9a91b466-7c46-5f76-b16e-9a0cfca603bb" 13194139536062 true] [17592186381694 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2(x+1){e}^{x}-(x+1)^{2}({e}^{x})}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+2-{x}^{2}-2x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-x)(1+x)}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-1,1,$ which coincide with the endpoints of the given interval.\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\frac{(-1+1)^{2}}{{e}^{-1}}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{(1+1)^{2}}{{e}^{1}}=\\frac{4}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=\\frac{4}{e},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{4}{e}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-1)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/e21d39f09f56fa1113184ce7a778d98561b82b932cd324ee90cf3aaff4d729d